1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,792
This is ECE 320, video 10 a. We fill pipeline diagrams for past exam questions, one cycle at a time.

2
00:00:10,242 --> 00:00:15,255
After this video, you can fill a pipeline diagram with and without forwarding.

3
00:00:15,705 --> 00:00:19,801
You can explain every stall, every forward and every flush.

4
00:00:20,251 --> 00:00:31,558
And you can fill the forwarding table and the stall table. These questions carry the largest part of the midterm marks. Video 10 b continues with more questions.

5
00:00:32,008 --> 00:00:51,869
The method has five steps. Go through the instructions in program order. Step 1: for each source register, find the producer. The producer is the latest older instruction that writes this register. Skip flushed rows, and skip x0. If there is no producer, there is no hazard.

6
00:00:52,319 --> 00:01:01,749
Step 2: find when the value exists. An ALU result exists at the end of X. A load result exists only at the end of M.

7
00:01:02,199 --> 00:01:17,111
Step 3: find when the value is needed. An ALU input or an address is needed in X. Store data is needed in M. A branch with no forwarding into its compare unit needs its operands in D.

8
00:01:17,561 --> 00:01:27,182
Step 4: test if a path exists in the same cycle. The slides say that the from stage and the to stage of a path are in the same cycle.

9
00:01:27,632 --> 00:01:34,864
MX works when the consumer is in X and the producer is in M. The producer must not be a load.

10
00:01:35,314 --> 00:01:40,349
WX works when the consumer is in X and the producer is in W.

11
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:47,327
WM is for store data only. The store is in M, and the producer is in W.

12
00:01:47,777 --> 00:01:53,131
The register file works when the consumer is in D after the producer's W.

13
00:01:53,581 --> 00:01:59,277
If a path exists, the value is forwarded. You write one row in the forwarding table.

14
00:01:59,727 --> 00:02:11,461
If no path exists, the consumer stalls in D. D repeats, and the repeat gets a star. The instruction behind it repeats F. In the next cycle, test again.

15
00:02:11,911 --> 00:02:28,529
Step 5 is for branches. A branch resolves in X. The pipeline guesses not taken. If the branch is taken, the 2 younger instructions are flushed. They get a dash. The target is fetched in the next cycle. This costs 2 cycles.

16
00:02:28,979 --> 00:02:41,545
Now the two times in real diagrams. On the left, add writes x1. It computes x1 in X, in cycle 3. So the value exists at the end of cycle 3.

17
00:02:41,995 --> 00:02:56,651
The next instruction, sub, reads x1. sub is in X in cycle 4, and add is in M. So x1 goes by MX. The green arrow is vertical, because both stages are in the same cycle.

18
00:02:57,101 --> 00:03:04,866
Two instructions later, and is in X in cycle 5. add is in W. So x1 goes by WX.

19
00:03:05,316 --> 00:03:25,156
Three instructions later, or is in D in cycle 5. In the same cycle, add writes x1 in W. There is no forwarding into D. D sees only values written in earlier cycles. So or reads the old x1, and D repeats once. This is the W D case.

20
00:03:25,606 --> 00:03:30,022
In cycle 6, or reads the new x1 from the register file.

21
00:03:30,472 --> 00:03:40,861
On the right, the producer is a load, and sub reads x1. Without a stall, sub would be in X in cycle 4. In cycle 4, the load is in M.

22
00:03:41,311 --> 00:03:56,266
The load value exists only at the end of M, in cycle 4. So sub cannot get it in X in cycle 4, even with MX. This is the load-use case. sub stalls one cycle: D star in cycle 3.

23
00:03:56,716 --> 00:04:03,777
In cycle 5, sub is in X and the load is in W. So sub gets x1 by WX.

24
00:04:04,227 --> 00:04:19,182
Now the instruction and. It is 2 instructions after the load, but the stall moved it. Its D is now in cycle 5, the cycle of the load's W. This is the W D case again, so it stalls once more.

25
00:04:19,632 --> 00:04:27,739
In cycle 6, and reads x1 from the register file. Rule: after any stall, compare the real cycles again.

26
00:04:28,189 --> 00:04:44,637
The table gives the path, or the number of stalls, for each distance. Each cell has its own small diagram. First row: an ALU result used as an ALU input or an address. One instruction later, the path is MX.

27
00:04:45,087 --> 00:04:46,836
Two later: WX.

28
00:04:47,286 --> 00:04:55,158
Three later: one stall. The consumer's D is in the cycle of the producer's W. This is the W D case.

29
00:04:55,608 --> 00:05:01,453
Four or more later: the consumer reads the register file in D. There is no stall.

30
00:05:01,903 --> 00:05:09,839
Second row: a load result used in the ALU. One later: one stall, then WX. This is load-use.

31
00:05:10,289 --> 00:05:12,039
Two later: WX.

32
00:05:12,489 --> 00:05:15,667
Three later: one stall, the W D case.

33
00:05:16,117 --> 00:05:18,443
Four later: the register file.

34
00:05:18,893 --> 00:05:24,738
Third row: store data. The store needs the data in M. One later: WM.

35
00:05:25,188 --> 00:05:37,028
Two later: two stalls. Store data has no WX path in the class datapath. So the store must read the register file in D, after the W of the producer.

36
00:05:37,478 --> 00:05:40,657
Three later: one stall, the W D case.

37
00:05:41,107 --> 00:05:43,432
Four later: the register file.

38
00:05:43,882 --> 00:05:58,602
Last row: no forwarding at all, as in Assignment 3 part a. The same holds for a branch with no compare forwarding. The consumer reads in D after the producer's W. One later: 3 stalls.

39
00:05:59,052 --> 00:06:00,780
Two later: 2 stalls.

40
00:06:01,230 --> 00:06:03,107
Three later: 1 stall.

41
00:06:03,557 --> 00:06:05,328
Four later: no stall.

42
00:06:05,778 --> 00:06:14,119
Use the table only when no stall comes between the two instructions. After a stall, compare the real cycles again.

43
00:06:14,569 --> 00:06:24,148
In the load example, and is 2 instructions after the load. The table says WX. But the stall moved and, and now it stalls once.

44
00:06:24,598 --> 00:06:37,846
Now the notation. Slide 47 shows a load-use stall. add stays in D in cycle 3. That cycle is written D star. A star marks every repeat except the last one.

45
00:06:38,296 --> 00:06:50,819
The instruction behind it, sub, cannot enter D in cycle 3. So it repeats F. That cycle is written F star. Every D stall gives an F stall to the next instruction.

46
00:06:51,269 --> 00:06:59,290
Part 2, slide 4, shows a taken branch. B E Q is in X in cycle 4. It resolves there: taken.

47
00:06:59,740 --> 00:07:08,871
The pipeline guessed not taken, so it fetched store word and add. These 2 instructions are flushed. They get a dash in their later cycles.

48
00:07:09,321 --> 00:07:19,582
The target is fetched in cycle 5, the cycle after X. The 2 flushed rows are 2 lost cycles. So a taken branch costs 2 cycles.

49
00:07:20,032 --> 00:07:25,301
Our first question is Assignment 3, question 4. There are 6 instructions.

50
00:07:25,751 --> 00:07:28,119
In part a, there is no forwarding.

51
00:07:28,569 --> 00:07:33,519
In part b, there is full forwarding, also into the branch compare unit.

52
00:07:33,969 --> 00:07:39,771
All memory accesses hit, and the branch is not taken. Pause the video and try it.

53
00:07:40,221 --> 00:07:47,560
Step 1 of the method: find the producers. S L T reads t2. The load, number 1, writes t2.

54
00:07:48,010 --> 00:07:53,322
B E Q reads t1, which S L T writes. The branch is not taken.

55
00:07:53,772 --> 00:07:55,799
sub reads t2 from the load.

56
00:07:56,249 --> 00:08:01,795
add reads t2. The producer is sub, the latest older writer of t2.

57
00:08:02,245 --> 00:08:06,960
store word uses t2 as its base address. The producer is add.

58
00:08:07,410 --> 00:08:12,914
store word also reads t3, and so does the load. Nothing writes t3.

59
00:08:13,364 --> 00:08:24,564
Part a has no forwarding. In cycles 1 and 2, the load is fetched and decoded. In D it reads t3. Nothing older writes t3, so there is no hazard.

60
00:08:25,014 --> 00:08:33,782
In cycle 3, S L T is in D. It needs t2. The load writes t2 only in W, in cycle 5.

61
00:08:34,232 --> 00:08:44,451
There is no forwarding. So S L T must read t2 in D after cycle 5. S L T repeats D in cycles 3, 4 and 5.

62
00:08:44,901 --> 00:08:50,853
B E Q is behind S L T. B E Q repeats F in the same cycles.

63
00:08:51,303 --> 00:09:02,481
In cycle 6, S L T reads t2 from the register file. The load wrote it in cycle 5, an earlier cycle. So S L T gets the new value.

64
00:09:02,931 --> 00:09:06,281
B E Q does its last F in cycle 6.

65
00:09:06,731 --> 00:09:16,629
In cycle 7, B E Q is in D. It needs t1 from S L T. S L T writes t1 in W in cycle 9.

66
00:09:17,079 --> 00:09:21,133
So B E Q repeats D in cycles 7, 8 and 9.

67
00:09:21,583 --> 00:09:23,972
sub waits in F in the same cycles.

68
00:09:24,422 --> 00:09:29,030
In cycle 10, B E Q reads t1 from the register file.

69
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:34,877
In cycle 11, B E Q is in X and resolves. It is not taken.

70
00:09:35,327 --> 00:09:41,343
The pipeline guessed not taken. So sub is the right next instruction, and nothing is flushed.

71
00:09:41,793 --> 00:09:45,185
The add at the label SKIP is fetched in cycle 11.

72
00:09:45,635 --> 00:09:54,553
sub reads t2 in D in cycle 11. The load wrote t2 in cycle 5, long before. So sub has no stall.

73
00:09:55,003 --> 00:10:00,656
add needs t2 from sub. sub writes t2 in W in cycle 14.

74
00:10:01,106 --> 00:10:05,159
So add repeats D in cycles 12, 13 and 14.

75
00:10:05,609 --> 00:10:08,511
store word waits in F in the same cycles.

76
00:10:08,961 --> 00:10:15,958
store word uses t2 as its base address. add writes t2 in W in cycle 18.

77
00:10:16,408 --> 00:10:21,165
So store word repeats D in cycles 16, 17 and 18.

78
00:10:21,615 --> 00:10:26,373
In cycle 19, store word reads t2 from the register file.

79
00:10:26,823 --> 00:10:36,551
store word finishes W in cycle 22. So the run takes 22 cycles. The diagram is the same as the published solution.

80
00:10:37,001 --> 00:10:51,529
Count the D star cells. There are 4 RAW hazards, that is, read after write hazards. Each is between neighbours, and each gives 3 D star cells. 4 times 3 is 12 stall cycles.

81
00:10:51,979 --> 00:11:04,288
Part b has full forwarding. In cycle 3, S L T is in D, and it needs t2 from the load. Its X would be cycle 4. In cycle 4, the load is in M.

82
00:11:04,738 --> 00:11:16,002
The load value exists only at the end of M. So MX cannot help. This is the load-use case. S L T stalls one cycle: D star in cycle 3.

83
00:11:16,452 --> 00:11:19,289
B E Q repeats F in cycle 3.

84
00:11:19,739 --> 00:11:28,251
In cycle 5, S L T is in X and the load is in W. t2 goes by WX into the ALU.

85
00:11:28,701 --> 00:11:41,416
In cycle 6, B E Q is in X and S L T is in M. t1 goes by MX into the compare unit. The question allows forwarding into the branch compare unit.

86
00:11:41,866 --> 00:11:48,159
B E Q resolves in X in cycle 6. It is not taken, so nothing is flushed.

87
00:11:48,609 --> 00:12:00,812
sub needs t2 from the load. sub is in D in cycle 6. The load wrote t2 in cycle 5. So the register file already has it. No forward is needed.

88
00:12:01,262 --> 00:12:06,958
In cycle 8, add is in X and sub is in M. t2 goes by MX.

89
00:12:07,408 --> 00:12:24,773
In cycle 9, store word is in X and add is in M. store word uses t2 as its address, and the address goes through the ALU. So t2 goes by MX, not by WM. The published solution has the same note.

90
00:12:25,223 --> 00:12:27,378
The run ends in cycle 11.

91
00:12:27,828 --> 00:12:39,049
Part a took 22 cycles. Two runs of 11 cycles fit in 22 cycles. So 22 over 11 is 2: forwarding gives a speedup of 2.

92
00:12:39,499 --> 00:12:43,062
Only the load-use stall is left: one D star.

93
00:12:43,512 --> 00:12:50,893
Now a real midterm question: fall 2019, question 2. It has 14 instructions.

94
00:12:51,343 --> 00:12:56,805
The pipeline has forwarding as in class, but no forwarding into the decode stage.

95
00:12:57,255 --> 00:13:04,593
Branch direction and target are known in X. The pipeline guesses not taken until the branch resolves.

96
00:13:05,043 --> 00:13:07,667
The branch in instruction 8 is taken.

97
00:13:08,117 --> 00:13:15,221
Part A asks for the pipeline diagram, the forwarding table and the stall table. It is worth 18 marks.

98
00:13:15,671 --> 00:13:27,383
Two facts of the class datapath decide many cells. First, MX and WX feed only the ALU inputs. So store data has only the WM path.

99
00:13:27,833 --> 00:13:39,055
Second, there is no forwarding into D. A D in the same cycle as the producer's W reads the old value. Pause the video and try it. Then continue.

100
00:13:39,505 --> 00:13:49,681
Step 1 of the method: write the producer of each source register. Number 1 and number 2 read x3 and x4. Nothing older writes them.

101
00:13:50,131 --> 00:13:55,997
Number 3 uses x3 as its base address. The producer is the load, number 2.

102
00:13:56,447 --> 00:14:07,050
Number 4 add also reads x3 from number 2. Orange means a load producer. That is a warning: a load value exists only at the end of M.

103
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:18,465
Number 5 and number 6 are stores. Each uses x6 two times: as the base address and as the store data. The producer is add number 4.

104
00:14:18,915 --> 00:14:22,499
Number 7 reads x8, and nothing older writes it.

105
00:14:22,949 --> 00:14:28,261
Number 8 B E Q reads x6 from number 4. The branch is taken.

106
00:14:28,711 --> 00:14:35,175
So number 9 and number 10 are on the wrong path. They are flushed, so they are not producers.

107
00:14:35,625 --> 00:14:45,396
Number 11 reads x7. Number 9 also writes x7, but number 9 is flushed. So the producer is the load number 7.

108
00:14:45,846 --> 00:14:51,862
Number 12 and number 13 read only x0. x0 is never a hazard.

109
00:14:52,312 --> 00:14:55,704
Number 14 reads x7 from number 11.

110
00:14:56,154 --> 00:15:07,354
Number 14 also reads x8. Number 12 and number 13 both write x8. Number 13 is later, so the load number 13 is the producer.

111
00:15:07,804 --> 00:15:19,857
Now fill the diagram. In cycles 1 to 3, number 1 store word and number 2 load word read x3 and x4. Nothing older writes them, so there is no hazard.

112
00:15:20,307 --> 00:15:32,297
In cycle 4, number 3 is in D. Its base x3 comes from the load number 2. The X of number 3 would be cycle 5. In cycle 5, the load is in M.

113
00:15:32,747 --> 00:15:40,747
The load value exists only at the end of M. This is load-use, so number 3 stalls: D star in cycle 4.

114
00:15:41,197 --> 00:15:44,013
Number 4 repeats F in cycle 4.

115
00:15:44,463 --> 00:15:54,404
In cycle 6, number 3 is in X and the load is in W. x3 goes by WX into the ALU, for the base address.

116
00:15:54,854 --> 00:16:12,902
In cycle 6, number 4 add is in D, and it also needs x3. The load writes x3 in cycle 6, but D sees only earlier writes. So number 4 reads the old x3. This is the W D case, and number 4 repeats D.

117
00:16:13,352 --> 00:16:16,232
Number 5 repeats F in cycle 6.

118
00:16:16,682 --> 00:16:23,551
In cycle 7, number 4 reads x3 again from the register file. Now it gets the new value.

119
00:16:24,001 --> 00:16:27,415
Number 5 does its last F in cycle 7.

120
00:16:27,865 --> 00:16:40,302
Number 5 store word uses x6 as base and as data. In cycle 9, number 5 is in X and add number 4 is in M. The base x6 goes by MX.

121
00:16:40,752 --> 00:16:46,064
Number 6 is in D in cycle 9, and it waits. The next steps show why.

122
00:16:46,514 --> 00:16:56,221
In cycle 10, number 5 is in M and number 4 is in W. The store data x6 of number 5 goes by WM.

123
00:16:56,671 --> 00:17:07,487
Now number 6, seen from cycle 9. It is also a store, with x6 as base and data. Its X would be cycle 10, while number 4 is in W.

124
00:17:07,937 --> 00:17:20,054
The base could use WX. But store data has no WX path, and WM would need number 6 in M in cycle 10. So number 6 stalls in cycle 9.

125
00:17:20,504 --> 00:17:30,232
In cycle 10, number 6 is in D in the cycle of number 4's W. That is the W D case, so number 6 repeats D again.

126
00:17:30,682 --> 00:17:34,117
Number 7 waits in F in cycles 9 and 10.

127
00:17:34,567 --> 00:17:39,025
In cycle 11, number 6 reads x6 from the register file.

128
00:17:39,475 --> 00:17:43,507
Count the D star cells of number 6: 2 stall cycles.

129
00:17:43,957 --> 00:17:47,456
Number 7 does its last F in cycle 11.

130
00:17:47,906 --> 00:17:55,223
In cycle 12, number 7 load word is in D. It reads x8, and nothing older writes x8.

131
00:17:55,673 --> 00:18:07,940
In cycle 13, number 8 B E Q is in D. It reads x6 and x8. Number 4 wrote x6 in cycle 10, long before. So there is no hazard.

132
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:13,126
In cycle 14, B E Q is in X. It resolves: taken.

133
00:18:13,576 --> 00:18:20,147
The pipeline guessed not taken, so it fetched number 9 and number 10. Both are on the wrong path.

134
00:18:20,597 --> 00:18:27,125
In cycle 15, number 9 and number 10 are flushed. They get a dash in each later cycle.

135
00:18:27,575 --> 00:18:33,911
The target T1, instruction 11, is fetched in cycle 15, the cycle after X.

136
00:18:34,361 --> 00:18:38,585
So the taken branch costs 2 cycles: the 2 flushed rows.

137
00:18:39,035 --> 00:18:55,824
Number 11 needs x7. Number 9 also writes x7, but number 9 is flushed. So the producer is the load number 7. It wrote x7 in cycle 15. Number 11 reads it in D in cycle 16. No stall.

138
00:18:56,274 --> 00:19:04,829
In cycles 17 and 18, number 12 and number 13 read only x0. x0 is never a hazard.

139
00:19:05,279 --> 00:19:18,292
Number 14 add needs x7 and x8. x8 comes from the load number 13. The X of number 14 would be cycle 20, when the load is in M. That is load-use.

140
00:19:18,742 --> 00:19:30,070
x7 comes from number 11. Number 11 is in W in cycle 19, the same cycle as the D of number 14. That is the W D case.

141
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:36,109
Both reasons ask for a stall in cycle 19. One D star covers both.

142
00:19:36,559 --> 00:19:41,466
In cycle 20, number 14 reads x7 from the register file.

143
00:19:41,916 --> 00:19:50,855
In cycle 21, number 14 is in X and the load number 13 is in W. x8 goes by WX.

144
00:19:51,305 --> 00:19:57,065
Number 14 finishes in cycle 23. So the run takes 23 cycles.

145
00:19:57,515 --> 00:20:00,864
Count the forwarding arrows. There are 4 forwards.

146
00:20:01,314 --> 00:20:05,474
Count the D star cells. There are 5 stall cycles in D.

147
00:20:05,924 --> 00:20:22,137
Now the forwarding table. Each forward is one row. Row 1: source number 2, destination number 3, register x3, from W to X, in cycle 6. The small diagram shows the two instructions in that cycle.

148
00:20:22,587 --> 00:20:31,654
Row 2: from number 4 to number 5, x6, from M to X, in cycle 9. This is the base address of number 5.

149
00:20:32,104 --> 00:20:41,448
Row 3: from number 4 to number 5 again, x6, from W to M, in cycle 10. This is the store data of number 5.

150
00:20:41,898 --> 00:20:50,389
Rows 2 and 3 have the same destination, number 5. One instruction can receive two forwards, one for each source.

151
00:20:50,839 --> 00:20:57,943
Row 4: from number 13 to number 14, x8, from W to X, in cycle 21.

152
00:20:58,393 --> 00:21:11,235
There is no forward for x7 into number 14. Number 11 writes x7 in cycle 19, and number 14 reads it from the register file in D, in cycle 20.

153
00:21:11,685 --> 00:21:23,120
Now the stall table. Each stall gets one row with its stage, its cycles and the reason. Number 3 stalls in D in cycle 4: load-use on x3.

154
00:21:23,570 --> 00:21:28,498
Because number 3 holds D, number 4 stalls in F in cycle 4.

155
00:21:28,948 --> 00:21:42,068
Number 4 stalls in D in cycle 6. The reason is the W D case. Number 2 writes x3 in W in cycle 6. D in the same cycle reads the old value.

156
00:21:42,518 --> 00:21:45,505
Number 5 stalls in F in cycle 6.

157
00:21:45,955 --> 00:22:00,824
Number 6 stalls in D in cycles 9 and 10. Its store data x6 has only the WM path, and WM is not possible in time. So number 6 reads x6 in D after cycle 10.

158
00:22:01,274 --> 00:22:04,687
Number 7 stalls in F in cycles 9 and 10.

159
00:22:05,137 --> 00:22:18,684
Number 14 stalls in D in cycle 19. There are two reasons: load-use on x8 from number 13, and the W D case on x7 from number 11. Write both reasons.

160
00:22:19,134 --> 00:22:27,241
The flushed instructions number 9 and number 10 do not go in the stall table. They go in the diagram, with dashes.

161
00:22:27,691 --> 00:22:36,821
The recap of video 10 a. One. For each source register, find the producer, the time the value exists, and the time it is needed.

162
00:22:37,271 --> 00:22:51,181
Two. A path works only when both stages are in the same cycle. MX, but not from a load. WX. WM for store data. Or the register file, after the producer's W.

163
00:22:51,631 --> 00:23:00,975
Three. If there is no path, the consumer stalls in D, and the instruction behind it stalls in F. Test again in the next cycle.

164
00:23:01,425 --> 00:23:09,851
Four. There is no forwarding into D. A D in the cycle of the producer's W reads the old value and stalls once.

165
00:23:10,301 --> 00:23:18,664
Five. In the class datapath, store data has only the WM path. Two instructions later, it costs 2 stalls.

166
00:23:19,114 --> 00:23:26,645
Six. A taken branch resolves in X. Two rows get dashes, and the target is fetched in the next cycle.

167
00:23:27,095 --> 00:23:40,940
Seven. After any stall, compare the real cycles again. Flushed rows are never producers. Video 10 b continues with Part B of this question and with the fall 2022 paper.
